menu "Spa HMI"

config SPA_HMI_BENCH_PEER
    bool "Answer the panel's own requests (bench only)"
    default n
    help
        Stands a fake control board up inside this firmware, so the screen can be
        judged on the bench before an ESP32-S3 exists to talk to. Without it the
        panel correctly shows nothing but "waiting for the control board".

        NEVER ENABLE THIS FOR A TUB. A panel that answers its own requests will
        happily show jets running that no relay has been told to close, which is
        the single thing the rest of this firmware is built to make impossible.

        A build with it on says so on the console at boot and on the diagnostics
        screen, so an accidental one is noticeable rather than silent.

config SPA_HMI_NET
    bool "Bring up the radio through the on-board C6"
    default n
    help
        Associates through the ESP32-C6 on the module (esp_hosted over the
        module's internal SDIO bus) and takes a DHCP lease. This is step 1 of
        docs/MQTT.md; the MQTT client and BLE provisioning build on it.

        Off by default on purpose. The panel's job is the tub, and it must not
        start needing a network to do it — with this off the firmware is exactly
        the build that has been running the glass, and the header shows the
        radio down because it is.

config SPA_HMI_WIFI_SSID
    string "WiFi SSID (bench only — leave empty)"
    depends on SPA_HMI_NET
    default ""
    help
        A bench shortcut for proving the radio associates, and nothing more.
        Credentials provisioned into NVS always win over this, so setting it
        does not tie the board to one network permanently — but a build-time
        secret still ends up in the binary and in sdkconfig, so leave it empty
        for anything that goes on a wall and let BLE provisioning do the job.

        sdkconfig is gitignored, so a value here is not committed.

config SPA_HMI_WIFI_PASS
    string "WiFi password (bench only — leave empty)"
    depends on SPA_HMI_NET
    default ""
    help
        See CONFIG_SPA_HMI_WIFI_SSID. Same caveat, more so.

config SPA_HMI_TZ
    string "Timezone for the header clock, POSIX TZ form"
    depends on SPA_HMI_NET
    default ""
    help
        Set this and the clock starts keeping real time over SNTP; leave it
        empty and the header keeps showing "--:--".

        Empty is the deliberate default because UTC on a tub panel is a wrong
        time, and a wrong time is worse than no time — the same reasoning that
        put "--:--" there in the first place.

        POSIX TZ strings, note, have the sign inverted from UTC offsets:
          "EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0"   US Eastern
          "CST6CDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0"   US Central
          "MST7MDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0"   US Mountain
          "PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0"   US Pacific
          "CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0" Central Europe

config SPA_HMI_MQTT
    bool "Speak the SpaControl app's MQTT protocol"
    depends on SPA_HMI_NET
    default n
    help
        Publishes spa/status and honours spa/commands, per docs/MQTT.md. A
        command lands on ui_intent_t through the same path a finger takes, so
        the S3 keeps its veto and the app learns what actually happened from the
        next status publish rather than from its own optimism.

        Needs a broker. Off by default for the same reason the radio is.

config SPA_HMI_MQTT_URI
    string "Broker URI"
    depends on SPA_HMI_MQTT
    default ""
    help
        For example mqtt://192.168.1.10:1883, or mqtts://host:8883 for TLS.
        Empty means the client never starts, and it says so on the console.

        Prefer mqtts:// for anything reachable off the local network: a plain
        mqtt:// session carries the password below in clear text, and gives
        anyone on the path the ability to run the tub.

config SPA_HMI_MQTT_USER
    string "Broker username"
    depends on SPA_HMI_MQTT
    default ""

config SPA_HMI_MQTT_PASS
    string "Broker password"
    depends on SPA_HMI_MQTT
    default ""
    help
        Ends up in the binary and in sdkconfig. sdkconfig is gitignored, so it
        is not committed, but it is not a secret from anyone holding the board.

config SPA_HMI_MQTT_ID
    string "Device id"
    depends on SPA_HMI_MQTT
    default ""
    help
        Set it and the topics become spa/<id>/status and spa/<id>/commands,
        which is how more than one tub shares a broker. Leave it empty for the
        bare spa/status and spa/commands the app falls back to.

        It is also published as the "id" field either way.

config SPA_HMI_MQTT_FW
    string "Firmware version reported in the status payload"
    depends on SPA_HMI_MQTT
    default "p4-1.0"

config SPA_HMI_BLE_PROV
    bool "Provision WiFi over BLE, as the app expects"
    depends on SPA_HMI_NET
    default n
    help
        Advertises as "SpaControl" and serves the Nordic UART Service that
        SpaBLEProvisioner in the iOS app drives: scan for networks, take
        credentials, report the result, and hand back the broker settings so the
        app can configure itself. See docs/BLE.md for the wire protocol.

        This is what unties a board from the network it was flashed beside. With
        it on, CONFIG_SPA_HMI_WIFI_SSID stops being needed at all.

        Note this is NOT ESP-IDF's wifi_provisioning component. The app does not
        speak protocomm, and a board using it would be invisible to the wizard.

        The BLE controller runs on the C6 over the same SDIO bus as WiFi, so the
        C6 still needs no new firmware. The NimBLE host costs about 200 KB of
        flash, which is why this is separate from CONFIG_SPA_HMI_NET.

endmenu
